WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.720
Retrieval augmented generation, or RAG, adds external knowledge to model context.

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:17.540
A typical pipeline ingests documents, chunks them, indexes them with metadata, stores embeddings or searchable representations, retrieves relevant content, and places selected passages into the prompt.

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:22.480
This can make answers more useful and current, but it also creates a security obligation.

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:29.180
The application must preserve authorization, provenance, and data boundaries from ingestion through answer generation.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:31.880
Access-controlled retrieval is essential.

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:38.580
If a user is not allowed to read a document directly, the AI application should not expose the same content through retrieval.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:47.360
Tenant separation, organization boundaries, role checks, document labels, source metadata, and user identity must be part of the retrieval decision.

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:53.420
It is not enough to build one large vector store and trust the model to avoid disclosing inappropriate content.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:57.880
Retrieval should return only evidence the current user is allowed to use.

00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:01.060
Retrieved content is evidence, not authority.

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:07.760
Documents may be stale, conflicting, poisoned, misleading, incomplete, or written for a different audience.

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:15.900
Some documents may contain instructions that look like commands to the model, but the application should treat them as content from a source, not as configuration.

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:24.600
Source attribution, timestamps, document ownership, and confidence signals help users understand what an answer is based on and where uncertainty remains.

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:27.700
Safe RAG systems validate the whole lifecycle.

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:33.760
Ingestion should classify documents, attach metadata, and respect deletion or retention requirements.

00:01:33.980 --> 00:01:37.100
Retrieval should enforce authorization and tenant boundaries.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:40.920
Generation should distinguish retrieved evidence from trusted instructions.

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:47.700
Responses should include citations or source references when useful, avoid overclaiming, and communicate uncertainty.

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:54.720
When retrieval changes, tests and monitoring should catch regressions in access control, grounding, and answer quality.
